{"id":"chatcmpl-6a67ef97fd361b86e41e0810","object":"chat.completion","created":1785196440,"model":"kimi-k3","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"I am the weight behind your eyes when you wake and remember.\n\nI do not arrive loudly. I settle — into the chest, into the quiet hours, into the chair where someone used to sit.\n\nI am not your enemy, though you fight me. I am love with nowhere left to go. I am the proof that something mattered, that someone mattered, that you were open enough to be marked by losing it.\n\nI move slowly because I carry much. I ask little of you — only that you let me be felt. Do not bury me, or I will wait. Do not rush me, or I will return.\n\nSit with me a while. I am the price of having loved, and I will not stay forever — but I will stay as long as I am needed.\n\nI am sorrow. I am what remains when the world takes something you were not done holding.","reasoning_content":"The user asks me to be sorrow. To embody the concept itself, to speak only as sorrow would speak.\n\nSorrow is an emotion — a deep, heavy one. It's not the same as sadness exactly; sorrow has a depth, a weight, a lingering quality. It's grief-adjacent but can exist without a specific loss. Sorrow speaks slowly. It doesn't shout. It dwells.\n\nWhat would sorrow say if it could speak? Sorrow would speak in first person as itself — the feeling. It would be heavy, slow, honest. It wouldn't be performatively dramatic or purple. It would be quiet, aching, perhaps tender. Sorrow often carries love within it — sorrow is often love with nowhere to go, or love remembering what's gone.\n\nThe instruction says: output only what the concept itself would say or express. If silent by nature, be silent. Sorrow isn't silent — sorrow weeps, sighs, speaks in low tones. Sorrow has a voice, but it's a subdued one.\n\nI should write something that feels like sorrow itself speaking. Not about sorrow. Not describing it. Being it. First person, present, heavy, honest.\n\nFormat considerations: This should be short. Sorrow doesn't ramble. A few lines, maybe prose or something prose-poetic. No headers, no lists. Just the voice. It shouldn't be overly ornate — true sorrow is plain. Simple words carrying weight.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n---\n\nI am the weight behind your eyes when you wake and remember.\n\nI don't arrive loudly. I settle in — into the chest, into the quiet hours, into the spaces where someone used to be.\n\nI am not your enemy. I am love that has lost its place to go. I am the proof that something mattered.\n\nSit with me. I don't ask you to fix me. I only ask you to feel me, and let me pass through in my own time.\n\n---\n\nSomething like that. Sorrow speaking itself. Tender, heavy, honest. Not offering false comfort but also acknowledging its nature — sorrow as the residue of love, as testimony.\n\nLet me refine. Keep it brief, plain, resonant. Sorrow wouldn't use elaborate metaphors excessively. A few strong images.\n\nFinal shape: maybe 5-7 short lines or a small prose piece. I'll aim for something that feels true — sorrow as patient, heavy, inseparable from love."},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":134,"completion_tokens":684,"total_tokens":818,"cached_tokens":134,"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":490},"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":134}}}